==============================================================================
PART A -- the telescoping lemma, verified
==============================================================================
  If P's charge support is ONE-SIDED (all charges >= 0), then
        E[P^m] = L(p_0^m)   where p_0 = charge-0 part, L(v^k) = k!.
  Random test: build P with charges in {0,1,2}, compare both sides.

  trial 0: charges [0, 1, 2]  E[P^m]=['11', '1425', '719195']...  L(p0^m)=['11', '1425', '719195']...  True
  trial 1: charges [0, 2]  E[P^m]=['1', '5', '29']...  L(p0^m)=['1', '5', '29']...  True
  trial 2: charges [0, 1]  E[P^m]=['-3', '10', '-38']...  L(p0^m)=['-3', '10', '-38']...  True
  trial 3: charges [0, 1]  E[P^m]=['3', '14', '-70']...  L(p0^m)=['3', '14', '-70']...  True
  trial 4: charges [0, 2]  E[P^m]=['-13', '5697', '-8702109']...  L(p0^m)=['-13', '5697', '-8702109']...  True
  trial 5: charges [0, 1]  E[P^m]=['3', '14', '-70']...  L(p0^m)=['3', '14', '-70']...  True
  telescoping lemma holds on all trials: True

  Sanity: it FAILS when charges are two-sided (as it must -- that is exactly
  where the known counterexamples live).
    P = Z + W - ZW:  E[P^m] = ['-1', '4', '-18', '108']   L(p0^m) = ['-1', '2', '-6', '24']   equal? False

==============================================================================
PART B -- the reduced core: is there a NONZERO p with L(p^m) = 0 for all m?
==============================================================================
  L(f) = int_0^infty f(v) e^{-v} dv, i.e. L(v^k) = k!.

  deg p = 1: solving L(p^m)=0 for m=1..2 with a1=1  ->  0 solution(s)
      => NO nonzero p of degree 1.  (system is inconsistent)
  deg p = 2: solving L(p^m)=0 for m=1..3 with a2=1  ->  0 solution(s)
      => NO nonzero p of degree 2.  (system is inconsistent)
  deg p = 3: solving L(p^m)=0 for m=1..4 with a3=1  ->  0 solution(s)
      => NO nonzero p of degree 3.  (system is inconsistent)

  Asymptotic reason, checked numerically:  L(p^m) is dominated by the top
  coefficient, |L(p^m)| ~ |a_D|^m (Dm)! -- the saddle of |p(v)|^m e^{-v} sits at
  v ~ Dm, where the phase correction m*arg(p(v)/v^D) = O(1) and cannot oscillate
  the integral away.  So L(p^m) != 0 for large m whenever deg p >= 1.

                     p   m                     L(p^m)     L(p^m)/(a_D^m (Dm)!)
                 v - 1   2                          1                      0.5
                 v - 1   4                          9                    0.375
                 v - 1   6                        265       0.3680555555555556
                 v - 1   8                      14833      0.36788194444444444
              v^2-3v+2   2                          6                     0.25
              v^2-3v+2   4                       8840      0.21924603174603174
              v^2-3v+2   6                  105673552       0.2206121065148843
              v^2-3v+2   8              4629787579776      0.22127964791308044
                     v   2                          2                      1.0
                     v   4                         24                      1.0
                     v   6                        720                      1.0
                     v   8                      40320                      1.0

==============================================================================
PART C -- what this closes, and what it does not
==============================================================================
  CLOSED (modulo PART B): the ONE-SIDED-CHARGE branch of GMC(2).  If P's charge
  support is one-sided then E[P^m] = L(p_0^m); if no nonzero p has L(p^m)=0 for
  all m, then p_0 = 0, so P has NO charge-0 part, so P^m has no charge-0 part for
  any m, and E[Q P^m] = 0 as soon as m exceeds the charge budget of Q.  GMC holds.

  STILL OPEN: the TWO-SIDED case, where P has charges of both signs.  That is
  exactly where the n>=3 counterexamples live -- P = (1+Z)(W - g(Z)U) has charges
  from -1 up.  So two-sidedness is NECESSARY for failure, which is itself a
  structural constraint worth recording.
